[Page:Levels]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(LevelsContent)

[LevelsContent]
<h2 class="ref-section" id="what-a-level-is">What a level is</h2>

<div class="paragraph">
The level set is held in these tables. Each carries 100 records, one per level index,
all in the same order. After the hundredth level the game plays the set again
from the first record, while the LEVEL caption counts on:
</div>

#TABLE(default,centre,num)
{ =h Table | =h Record | =h What the record holds }
{ #R$6754(Level layouts) | 81 bytes | The 9×9 grid of brick slot codes, row-major }
{ #R$86F8(Spawn cells) | 4 bytes | Four candidate spawn cells, which may repeat }
{ #R$8888(Wave scripts) | 8 bytes | The level's wave script }
TABLE#


<div class="paragraph">
This page describes the level set shipped in the game image. The options
screen's #R$C1A0(LOAD LEVELS row) replaces it from tape. #LINK(krakout-ii)(Krakout II)
ships level layouts of its own.
</div>

<h2 class="ref-section" id="level-by-level">Level by level</h2>

<div class="paragraph">
One card per level, in level order. The picture is the
level's composed brick field, with its candidate spawn cells outlined by this
page. Under
it is the level's #R$8888(wave script). It shows one icon per
distinct object the slots hold, against the number of slots that hold it. It shows
empty&#215;N for the slots with the value that spawns nothing. Each icon is the object's own sprite, cataloged with its
frames and behavior on the #LINK(Graphics)(game graphics) page.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
Each picture composes the layout record through the #R$921A(tile-glyph groups) and #R$9191(brick attributes), with
the #R$D900(tile glyphs). They are drawn at 1:1 in the bat-on-right orientation.
Grid column 0 is the leftmost column on screen, and grid row 0 the topmost.
</div>

